      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "The degree of the remainder is less than the degree of the divisor.  So, the possible remainders are of the forms \\[_2x^{\\large\\pmod{2}},_2x^{7}-6x^{2},\\] and \\[x^{6}+11x+14,\\text{ }11x^2-11+2x,] so \\[whenever\\ the\\ degree\\ of\\ the\\ divisor,\\ deg \\ifmmode \\text{Remainder} \\else \\text{Remainder}\\ fi =0,\\] the degree of the remainder is $0.\\ \\ifmmode\\text{A} \\else \\text{A}\\ fi,\\ifmmode \\text{B} \\else \\text{B}\\ fi,\\ifmmode \\text{C} \\else \\text{C}\\ fi]\\ box.[\\begin{array}{ccccc} A:& 1 & 9 & 8 & 2 & 4 \\\\ B:& 1 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ C:& 1 & 2 & 10 & 9 & 8 \\end{array}\\] To determine the possible degrees of the remainder when a polynomial \\( P(x) \\) is divided by \\( 2x^2 - 7x + 18 \\), we need to consider the properties of polynomial division. Specifically, the degree of the remainder must be less than the degree of the divisor.\n\n1. **Degree of the Divisor**: \n   The polynomial we are dividing by is \\( 2x^2 - 7x + 18 \\). The degree of this polynomial is 2.\n\n2. **Possible Degrees of the Remainder**:\n   According to the properties of polynomial division:\n   - If the degree of the divisor is 2, then the degree of the remainder must be 0 or less than 2.\n   - Thus, the possible degrees of the remainder are 0 and 1.\n\n3. **Form of the Remainder**:\n   The possible forms of the remainder when dividing by a constant polynomial (degree 0) or a polynomial of degree less than 2 (degree 1) are:\n   - Constant form: \\( ax + b \\)\n   - Linear form: \\( cx + d \\)\n\nTherefore, the possible degrees of the remainder are \\( 0 \\) and \\( 1 \\).\n\nPutting it all together, the degrees of the possible remainders are:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0, 1}\n\\]",
